{{#is_alert}}**Workflow `{{workflow.name}}` on `{{host.name}}` is slow.**
Average execution duration over the last 15 minutes is **{{value}}s** (SLO: {{threshold}}s).
**Investigation checklist** (use the n8n Overview dashboard, filtered to this workflow and host):
1. **One workflow or all?** In *Workflow Performance*, look at the execution duration chart. If only this workflow climbed, the problem is in the workflow itself; if multiple workflows climbed together, suspect runtime or downstream.
2. **Which node is slow?** In *Node Execution*, find the `node_type` whose `started` rate climbed without a matching `finished` rate — usually an external HTTP node timing out.
3. **Runtime saturated?** In *Node.js Runtime Health*, check event loop lag p99. Sustained values above 100ms mean the Node.js process is the bottleneck.
4. **Queue backed up?** In *Queue & Worker Health*, a rising `waiting` gauge with a flat `dequeued` rate means workers can't keep up — scale workers or raise `N8N_CONCURRENCY_PRODUCTION_LIMIT`.
5. **Logs**: filter the *Logs* group to this workflow to identify the slow node by name.{{/is_alert}}

{{#is_warning}}Workflow `{{workflow_id.name}}` on `{{host.name}}` average duration approaching SLO: **{{value}}s** (warning at {{warn_threshold}}s).{{/is_warning}}
{{#is_recovery}}Workflow `{{workflow_id.name}}` average duration recovered to **{{value}}s**, below SLO.{{/is_recovery}}
